From 427e9d74d5bd57267afb876f260cf6a9a5f5adea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VERHILLE Arnaud <gist974@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 07:46:20 +0400
Subject: [PATCH 19/19] MMU: a misaligned access that crosses a page translates
 its second page

readBus() and writeBus() translated the first byte of an access only and
reached the rest at phys + 2 (read2x16, write2x16) or phys + 1 (an odd
word). When a misaligned word or long word crosses into a page that is not
physically contiguous, its second part read or wrote the page that follows
the first one physically. With logical page $10000000 mapped to $33000 and
$10001000 to $35000, MOVE.L (A0),D1 with A0 = $10000FFE read $AEAFA0A1
(the bytes at $34000) instead of $AEAF5051, MOVE.L D1,(A0) wrote $34000,
and an invalid second page took no exception at all. The 68030 behaved the
same.

MC68040UM 8.4.6.2: MA "is set if an ATC fault occurs for second-page
access that spans two pages in memory", and SIZE "corresponds to the
original access size"; 8.4.6.4: the FA "contains the address of the first
byte of the transfer, regardless of which of the two or three bus
transfers for the misaligned access was faulted". MC68030UM 9: "As the MMU
receives each logical address from the CPU core, it searches the ATC", a
general sentence that does not name a crossing access.

Pages are aligned and at least 256 bytes long (MC68030UM 9.7.2, PS; 4 or
8 Kbytes on the 68040, MC68040UM 3.1), so only an access that runs past a
256-byte boundary can cross one, where it enters its second long word.
For such an access, with the MMU enabled, readBus() and writeBus() now
translate that long word too (translateAcross()); if it does not follow
the first one physically, readAcross() and writeAcross() carry out the
access in words, a word whose bytes lie on different pages in two bytes.
Every other access takes the old path with no second lookup. An ATC fault
on the second page stacks MA and the first byte's logical address on a
68040. On a 68030 the frame keeps the logical address of the second page,
where a PTEST finds the fault, and the original size; MC68030UM 8.2 does
not describe this case, and Figure 8-9 can also be read as the remaining
size of the faulted cycle.

The bus order is not the processor's. MC68040UM 4.1: "If a misaligned
access crosses two pages, then the partial access to the first page
always happens twice". Here both pages are translated before the first
data cycle and the first part is transferred once, or not at all when the
second page faults: a peripheral on the first page sees one access, not
two.
